WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.060
European companies will test lunar rovers inside new Italian simulation facilities. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 220. According to I-Space, I-Space Europe SA and ALTEC SPA, put their names on a memorandum of understanding dated the 12th of October. That paper is meant to open a path toward joint work on lunar rover testing, mission simulations and operational training. The signing took place at the 77th International Astronautical Congress in

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:30.040
in Antalya, Turkey.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.800
Benchenzo Giorgio, chief executive of Altec,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.840
and Julian Alexandra Lamami,

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.160
chief executive of ISpace Europe,

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.080
both took part in the ceremony.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:41.520
Under the agreement,

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.160
the two organizations will evaluate opportunities

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.800
for ISpace Europe to use Altec facilities

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.220
for simulating rover platform operations

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.640
inside lunar analog environments.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.460
Plant activities include functional testing

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:57.380
of lunar rover platforms and ancillary devices.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:02.760
services. They also include mission simulations, operator rehearsals, and verification from

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:07.520
the subsystem level up through the complete vehicle. Targeted training and certification

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:13.220
for ISpace Europe's Lunar Mission Operations team sit on that list as well. The Memorandum

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:18.940
also opens discussion of broader cooperation on lunar exploration technologies, lunar mobility

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:24.660
systems, and space resource utilization. Altech is a public-private company based in

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:28.700
in and owned by Thales Alania Space and the Italian Space Agency.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:34.740
Since 2019, Altec has run the Rover Operations Control Center that supports ground testing

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:37.900
for ESA's Exo Mars Rosalind Franklin Rover.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:43.100
That heritage includes turning what happens in a simulator into procedures a flight team

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.080
can actually use.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:50.000
The company is now building a new lunar simulation center, funded by the Italian Space Agency,

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.660
to provide analog environments for testing robotic lunar exploration technologies and operational

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:56.360
concepts.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:01.700
For iSpace Europe, the partnership increases access to specialized European testing and

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:06.500
operations infrastructure, while the company develops its family of lunar rover systems

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.600
and future surface mobility missions.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:14.160
The cosmic spotlight on this agreement is about what that new center is for, and who already

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:15.740
knows how to use a room like it.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:21.120
The ice-based Europe and Altec agreement opens access to a dedicated lunar simulation

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.320
center that will replicate lunar surface conditions for rover hardware and operations teams.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:31.960
Altec already runs the control center for the Exel Mars rover, giving it direct experience

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:34.800
translating simulation results into flight procedures.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:40.240
The partnership could shorten the time needed to validate mobility systems and train operators

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.300
before any flight hardware reaches the moon.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:49.320
Future work may also examine technologies for lunar resource utilization once the simulation infrastructure is complete.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:58.500
The new center adds to Italy's growing role in planetary exploration through ASI funding and existing operational heritage with ESA missions.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:03.000
Cosmic deep dive, house-based craft match orbits during rendezvous.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.960
Picture two cars on a circular highway where one vehicle travels at 28,000 km per hour.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:11.920
Closing the gap is nothing like a straight road chase.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:17.740
The trailing craft has to speed up, climb into a higher lane, then slow down again so both

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:20.940
vehicles end up side by side at exactly the same speed.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:26.060
That sequence is what happens every time a capsule approaches the International Space Station.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.600
A spacecraft first enters a slightly lower orbit, where it travels faster relative to

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:32.100
the station.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:37.560
Then, it performs a series of burns to raise its path until the two trajectories coincide.

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.260
Each burn must be timed to within seconds.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:45.000
A one-minute error can leave the vehicle's hundreds of kilometers apart after a single

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:50.040
lap, hundreds of kilometers from a clock slip that short on a loop that never stops.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:55.920
The final approach occurs at relative speeds of only a few centimeters per second, so docking

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:57.820
mechanisms can engage without damage.

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:04.320
From 28,000 kilometers per hour down to a creep, you could watch with your own eyes if you were standing at the hatch.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:09.180
Even then, the job is not finished, because the small stuff refuses to sit still.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:16.540
Engineers still cannot predict exactly how small attitude control thruster firings will interact with the station's own micro movements.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:21.220
Those jitters come from solar panel flexing and from crew activity inside.

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:26.720
A puff from a thruster, a panel that bends, someone pushing off a bulkhead, all of

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.720
it lands in the same narrow closing gap.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:31.220
The geometry itself is not a mystery.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.040
We can see it work in every successful docking.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:39.080
The lanes can be matched, the burns can be stacked, and the last centimeters can be flown.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:43.380
What remains unknown is how much margin is actually left in that dance.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:48.820
Nobody knows precisely how much slack survives when the next generation of commercial vehicles

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:52.420
levels begins flying more frequent, higher traffic rendezvouss.

